---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Find What Font a Website Uses — 3 Easy Methods"
  description="Learn three practical ways to find out what font a website uses: inspecting with Chrome DevTools, using a font-inspector browser extension, and checking the page source. Step-by-step and free."
  path="/guides/how-to-find-what-font-a-website-uses/"
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-indigo-600">Home</a>
      <span class="mx-2 text-slate-300">/</span>
      <a href="/guides/" class="hover:text-indigo-600">Guides</a>
      <span class="mx-2 text-slate-300">/</span>
      <span class="text-slate-700">How to Find What Font a Website Uses</span>
    </nav>

    <article class="space-y-6">
      <header>
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl">
          How to Find What Font a Website Uses
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
          Unlike a flat image, a website's text is rendered live by your
          browser — which means the font name is sitting right there in the
          page's code. Here are three free methods to find it, from quickest to
          most thorough. All steps below are for Google Chrome on desktop; other
          Chromium browsers (Edge, Brave, Opera) work the same way.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Method 1: Inspect with DevTools (most reliable)</h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
          Your browser's built-in developer tools show exactly which font is
          rendering any piece of text. No extensions needed.
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-slate-700">
          <li>Open the website and find the text whose font you want to identify.</li>
          <li>Right-click directly on that text and choose <strong>Inspect</strong>. The DevTools panel opens with the corresponding HTML element highlighted.</li>
          <li>In the right-hand panel, make sure the <strong>Computed</strong> tab is selected (not "Styles").</li>
          <li>Scroll down to the <strong>font-family</strong> property. The first name listed is the font the page requests; the browser falls back to the later names only if the first is unavailable.</li>
          <li>To confirm what is actually rendering (not just requested), scroll further down in the Computed tab to the <strong>Rendered Fonts</strong> section at the bottom — it names the exact font file in use.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
          Tip: if the font name looks generic (like a system font stack), the
          site may be using the visitor's built-in system fonts rather than a
          named webfont. In that case there is no downloadable font to find —
          but you can match its look with a similar font from our
          <a href="/browse/" class="font-medium text-indigo-600 hover:underline">font browser</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Method 2: Use a font-inspector browser extension</h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
          If you do this often, a browser extension is faster than opening
          DevTools each time. <strong>WhatFont</strong> is a well-known free
          third-party extension for Chrome: after installing it from the Chrome
          Web Store, you click its toolbar icon and then hover over any text on
          the page to see the font name, size, weight, and line height in a
          small tooltip.
        </p>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
          A note of honesty: we have no affiliation with WhatFont or its
          developers. Extensions can occasionally misreport fonts on pages that
          load text as images or use heavy font substitution, so if a result
          looks suspicious, double-check it with Method 1.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Method 3: Check the page source for font links</h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
          Most websites load webfonts through a link in the page's HTML. To see
          them:
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 text-slate-700">
          <li>Right-click anywhere on the page (not on an image) and choose <strong>View page source</strong>.</li>
          <li>Press Ctrl+F (or Cmd+F on Mac) and search for <strong>"fonts.googleapis"</strong> or <strong>"font"</strong>.</li>
          <li>Look for <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm text-slate-800">&lt;link&gt;</code> tags pointing to font files — the URL usually contains the font family name (for example, <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm text-slate-800">family=Inter</code> means the site uses Inter).</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
          This method tells you which fonts the page loads, but not necessarily
          which one styles a specific headline — for that, Method 1 is more
          precise. It is still useful when DevTools feels like overkill and you
          just want the site's overall font list.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">What to do with the font name</h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
          Once you have the name, search for it in our
          <a href="/browse/" class="font-medium text-indigo-600 hover:underline">font browser</a>
          to preview it and check its weights and styles. If it turns out to be
          a paid commercial font, try our
          <a href="/identify/" class="font-medium text-indigo-600 hover:underline">Q&amp;A font finder</a>
          to discover a similar free alternative. And if all you have is a
          screenshot of the site rather than the live page, our guide on
          <a href="/guides/how-to-identify-a-font-from-an-image/" class="font-medium text-indigo-600 hover:underline">identifying a font from an image</a>
          covers that case.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
